Stage 3 PRD – Ngrok → Render Conversion
1. Objective

The goal of Stage 3 is to migrate the chat application from a local + ngrok tunnel setup to a cloud-hosted deployment on Render, providing:

A permanent public URL for both backend and frontend.

Access from anywhere on the internet, not restricted to the same Wi-Fi network.

No manual tunneling (like restarting ngrok).

Persistent uptime (as per Render’s free tier).

2. Current State

Frontend: Hosted locally (React/HTML/JS).

Backend: Node.js + Express + Socket.io running locally.

Ngrok: Used to expose local backend with a temporary public URL.

Limitations:

Ngrok generates a new URL each session.

Requires local server always running.

Works best when client + server are on the same local Wi-Fi network.

Not production-ready.

3. Target State

Backend: Node.js server deployed to Render Web Service (permanent HTTPS + WSS endpoint).

Frontend: Deployed to Render Static Site with permanent HTTPS URL.

Connectivity:

Clients connect to backend via Render public URL.

No dependency on local machine or ngrok.

Workflow: Push changes → GitHub → Render auto-build → Redeployed live.

4. How It Will Work

User Access:

User visits https://your-app-frontend.onrender.com.

Frontend loads in browser.

Chat Connection:

Frontend connects to backend WebSocket via wss://your-backend.onrender.com.

Messaging + File Upload:

Messages/files are sent through the Render-hosted backend.

Backend applies any validation (text, file size, steganography check, etc.).

Persistence:

Render hosts services 24/7.

No ngrok tunnels required.

5. Implementation Steps
Step 1 – Prepare Code

Backend changes

Remove localhost:3000 hardcoding.

Accept connections from all origins (CORS setup).

const cors = require("cors");
app.use(cors({ origin: "*" }));


Frontend changes

Replace WebSocket endpoint from:

const socket = io("http://localhost:3000");


to:

const socket = io("https://your-backend.onrender.com");

Step 2 – Deploy Backend to Render

Push backend code to GitHub repo.

Go to Render Dashboard
.

New → Web Service → Connect GitHub Repo.

Select Node.js environment.

Configure:

Build Command: npm install

Start Command: node index.js (or your main file)

Deploy.

Note down backend URL: https://your-backend.onrender.com.

Step 3 – Deploy Frontend to Render

Push frontend code (React/HTML/JS) to GitHub repo.

On Render → New → Static Site → Connect GitHub Repo.

Configure:

Build Command: npm run build (for React) or leave empty for pure HTML/JS.

Publish Directory: build/ (for React) or / for static files.

Deploy.

Note down frontend URL: https://your-frontend.onrender.com.

Step 4 – Connect Frontend to Backend

Update frontend WebSocket client to point to backend Render URL.

Redeploy frontend.

Test connectivity:

Open frontend URL in multiple devices/browsers.

Ensure chat + file uploads work.

6. Deliverables

Backend running on Render (permanent HTTPS + WSS URL).

Frontend running on Render (permanent HTTPS URL).

Users can connect & chat from any device with internet access.

No need for ngrok or same Wi-Fi network.

7. Risks & Mitigation

Free Tier Limitations:

Render free tier may spin down services after inactivity → Mitigation: Use paid plan if needed later.

File Size Limits:

Free tier max file size may restrict uploads → Mitigation: Add validation to restrict upload sizes.

Cold Start Delays:

Free services may take a few seconds to wake after idle → Acceptable for prototype stage.

8. Success Criteria

✅ Frontend + Backend hosted entirely on Render.

✅ Permanent URLs accessible globally.

✅ Chat & file sharing functional across devices/networks.

✅ No dependency on local server or ngrok